\ulnone\f0\fs28\lang1033 Q.1 \f1\lang9 Define Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) and its four main principles: Encapsulation,\f0\lang1033  \f1\lang9 Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\lang1033 ans.1  \b0 Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of objects, which represent real-world entities. These objects contain data (attributes) and methods (functions) that operate on the data. OOP helps in organizing complex programs, promoting code reusability, modularity, and maintainability.\par
\par
The four main principles of OOP are:\par
\par
Encapsulation:    It is the process of bundling data (variables) and methods (functions) that operate on that data into a single unit called a class.\par
\par
It hides the internal details of an object and only exposes what is necessary through public methods.\par
\par
Example: Using private variables and public getter/setter methods to control access.\par
Inheritance:\par
It allows a new class (child class or subclass) to acquire the properties and behaviors of an existing class (parent class or superclass).\par
\par
Promotes code reusability and establishes a relationship between classes.\par
\par
Example: A class Car can inherit from a class Vehicle.\par
\par
Polymorphism:\par
It means \f1\ldblquote\f0 many forms\f1\rdblquote  \f0 and allows one interface to be used for different data types or classes.\par
\par
The same method name can behave differently based on the object that invokes it.\par
\par
Example: A method draw() can perform differently for Circle, Rectangle, or Triangle objects.\par
\par
Abstraction:\par
It focuses on hiding the complex implementation details and showing only the essential features of an object.\par
\par
It simplifies code by allowing the programmer to interact with high-level operations instead of low-level details.\par
\par
Example: A user interacts with a print() method without knowing how the printing process works internally.\par
\par
\ul\b Class\ulnone\b0\par
\b Q.2    Explain the structure of a class in PHP, including properties and methods. \par
ans.2   \b0 In PHP, a class is a blueprint for creating objects. It defines the properties (variables) and methods (functions) that describe the behavior and characteristics of those objects.\par
\par
Structure of a Class in PHP\par
\par
A typical PHP class structure includes:\par
\par
Class Declaration\par
\par
Properties (Attributes)\par
\par
Methods (Functions)\par
\par
Object Creation (Instantiation)\par
\par
Syntax Example:\par
<?php\par
// Define a class\par
class Car \{\par
    // Properties (variables)\par
    public $brand;\par
    public $color;\par
\par
    // Constructor (special method to initialize properties)\par
    public function __construct($brand, $color) \{\par
        $this->brand = $brand;\par
        $this->color = $color;\par
    \}\par
\par
    // Method (function)\par
    public function displayInfo() \{\par
        echo "This car is a " . $this->color . " " . $this->brand . ".";\par
    \}\par
\}\par
\par
// Create an object (instance of the class)\par
$car1 = new Car("Toyota", "Red");\par
\par
// Access method\par
$car1->displayInfo();\par
?>\par
\par
Explanation of Components:\par
\par
class Keyword:\par
Used to define a new class.\par
\par
class Car \{ ... \}\par
\par
\par
Properties:\par
\par
Variables that store the data or state of an object.\par
\par
Defined inside the class but outside any method.\par
\par
Can have access modifiers like public, private, or protected.\par
\par
public $brand;\par
private $color;\par
\par
\par
Methods:\par
\par
Functions that define the behavior or actions of an object.\par
\par
Operate on the class\f1\rquote\f0 s properties using $this.\par
\par
public function displayInfo() \{\par
    echo $this->brand;\par
\}\par
\par
\par
Constructor (__construct()):\par
\par
A special method automatically called when an object is created.\par
\par
Used to initialize object properties.\par
\par
public function __construct($brand, $color) \{ ... \}\par
\par
\par
Object Creation:\par
\par
An object (instance) is created using the new keyword.\par
\par
$car1 = new Car("Toyota", "Red");\par
\par
