\ul\b Object\par
\ulnone Q.3   What is an object in OOP? Discuss how objects are instantiated from classes in PHP\par
ans.3   \b0 What is an Object in OOP?\par
In Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), an object is an instance of a class.\par
A class acts as a blueprint or template, and an object is a real entity created from that blueprint.\par
Each object has:\par
Properties (Attributes): Represent the object\f1\rquote\f0 s data or characteristics.\par
Methods (Functions): Define the actions or behaviors the object can perform.\par
For example:\par
If Car is a class, then a specific car like a red Toyota is an object of that class.\par
\f2\u-10179?\u-9143?\f1  \f0 Class = Template\par
\f2\u-10179?\u-9143?\f1  \f0 Object = Actual instance of that template\par
How Objects Are Instantiated from Classes in PHP\par
In PHP, objects are created (instantiated) from classes using the new keyword.\par
This process is known as instantiation.\par
Syntax:\par
$objectName = new ClassName();\par
If the class has a constructor, you can pass values to it:\par
$objectName = new ClassName(parameter1, parameter2);\par
Example:\par
<?php\par
// Define a class\par
class Car \{\par
    public $brand;\par
    public $color;\par
     // Constructor method (called automatically when object is created)\par
    public function __construct($brand, $color) \{\par
        $this->brand = $brand;\par
        $this->color = $color;\par
    \}\par
      // Method to display car details\par
    public function displayInfo() \{\par
        echo "This car is a " . $this->color . " " . $this->brand . ".";\par
    \}\par
\}\par
// Create (instantiate) objects from the class\par
$car1 = new Car("Toyota", "Red");\par
$car2 = new Car("Honda", "Blue");\par
// Access object methods\par
$car1->displayInfo();  // Output: This car is a Red Toyota.\par
echo "<br>";\par
$car2->displayInfo();  // Output: This car is a Blue Honda.\par
?>\par
Explanation:\par
class Car \{ ... \} \f1\emdash  Defines the class (blueprint).\par
new Car("Toyota", "Red"); \emdash  Creates an object (instance) of the Car class.\par
$car1 and $car2 \emdash  Are two different objects with their own property values.\par
$this keyword \emdash  Refers to the current object within the class.\f0  \par
Key Points:\par
